<p>In a deposit dispute over cleaning, the side with the clearer evidence usually wins. An independent adjudicator from the deposit protection scheme compares the check-in inventory with the check-out report, photos and receipts, then decides whether the property was returned in the same condition, allowing for fair wear and tear. Because the deposit is the tenant's money, the landlord has to show why any deduction is justified.</p>
<p>Cleaning is one of the most common causes of deposit disagreements, and many cases are lost not because one side was wrong but because they couldn't prove they were right.</p>
<p>We carry out end of tenancy cleans for tenants, landlords and letting agents across Hull and East Yorkshire, so we see both sides of these disputes. Here is what counts as evidence, how to gather it and the mistakes that sink otherwise good cases.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>The check-in inventory is the benchmark: the property should come back in the same condition, allowing for fair wear and tear.</li><li>Date-stamped, well-lit photos and video carry far more weight than written descriptions alone.</li><li>Landlords must show that any deduction is reasonable and backed by evidence, such as an itemised quote or invoice.</li><li>Most cleaning disputes can be avoided with a thorough clean, checked against the inventory, before the keys go back.</li></ul></div>
<h2>How deposit disputes over cleaning work</h2>
<p>In England, deposits for assured shorthold tenancies must be protected in a government-approved scheme (DPS, mydeposits or TDS) within 30 days of being received. At the end of the tenancy, landlord and tenant try to agree any deductions. If they can't, each scheme offers a free dispute resolution service.</p>
<p>The adjudicator doesn't visit the property. They decide from the written and photographic evidence each side submits, which is why the quality of your paperwork matters as much as the state of the oven. The government page on <a href="https://www.gov.uk/tenancy-deposit-protection" rel="noopener">tenancy deposit protection</a> explains the schemes and how they work.</p>
<p>Deductions have to be reasonable. A landlord can claim the cost of returning the property to its check-in standard, not of making it better than it was. Our guide on <a href="{{guide:how-to-get-your-deposit-back}}">getting your full deposit back</a> covers the tenant's side in more detail.</p>
<h2>What evidence counts in a cleaning dispute?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>The check-in inventory.</strong> A detailed report describing the cleanliness of each room and item at the start, ideally signed or acknowledged by the tenant. Without one, a landlord will find it very hard to show the property was dirtier at the end.</li>
<li><strong>The check-out report.</strong> Best done by the same clerk or in the same format, so like can be compared with like.</li>
<li><strong>Photos and video.</strong> Date-stamped, well lit and taken from similar angles at check-in and check-out.</li>
<li><strong>Invoices and receipts.</strong> A cleaning invoice showing what was done and when, or the landlord's quote or invoice for remedial cleaning.</li>
<li><strong>Correspondence.</strong> Emails or messages reporting problems at check-in, agreeing changes during the tenancy or responding to proposed deductions.</li>
</ul>
<p>The single most influential piece of evidence is usually the side-by-side comparison of check-in and check-out. Landlords can find out how to make that benchmark solid in our guide to <a href="{{guide:landlord-inventory-cleaning-standards}}">recording cleanliness at check-in</a>.</p>
<h2>Evidence for tenants: build your case before you hand back the keys</h2>
<ol>
<li>Find your check-in inventory and read the cleanliness notes for each room.</li>
<li>Clean to match it, using a <a href="{{guide:end-of-tenancy-cleaning-checklist}}">room-by-room end of tenancy checklist</a> so nothing is missed.</li>
<li>Once the property is empty and clean, take photos and a slow walk-through video of every room, including inside the oven, fridge and cupboards, the toilet, the shower and the carpets.</li>
<li>If you used a professional cleaner, keep the invoice. A professional <a href="{{service:end-of-tenancy-cleaning}}">end of tenancy clean</a> that follows the inventory, with an invoice listing the work, is strong evidence the property was left clean.</li>
<li>Attend the check-out if you can, and ask for a copy of the report.</li>
<li>If deductions are proposed, ask for the evidence and the costs in writing before agreeing to anything.</li>
</ol>
<p>Under the Tenant Fees Act 2019, a landlord or agent in England can't charge you a fee for professional cleaning or make it a condition of your tenancy. What matters is the condition you leave the property in, not who cleaned it. Our article on <a href="{{guide:professionally-cleaned-tenancy-clause}}">professional cleaning clauses in tenancy agreements</a> explains where the line sits.</p>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Keep the original photo and video files rather than screenshots or compressed copies sent through messaging apps. The original files hold the date and time they were taken. Shoot with the lights on and blinds open, because dark, blurry photos help nobody.</p></div>
<h2>Evidence for landlords and letting agents</h2>
<p>A landlord's evidence needs to show three things: the condition at check-in, the condition at check-out, and that the amount claimed is a reasonable cost of putting it right.</p>
<ul>
<li>Use a detailed inventory with clear cleanliness descriptions and photos, and give the tenant a chance to comment on it at the start.</li>
<li>Carry out the check-out promptly after the tenant leaves, before contractors or new tenants go in.</li>
<li>Photograph the same items from the same angles as at check-in.</li>
<li>Back up the claim with an itemised quote or invoice for the cleaning needed, rather than a round figure.</li>
<li>Take account of how long the tenancy lasted and what counts as fair wear and tear before you claim.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our cleaners follow letting-agent and inventory checklists, and we can invoice landlords and agents directly, which leaves a clear paper trail if a deduction is challenged. Our <a href="{{page:landlords}}">landlord services page</a> explains how we work with individual landlords and portfolios.</p>
<h2>Fair wear and tear or a cleaning problem?</h2>
<p>Adjudicators distinguish between dirt, which a tenant is expected to clean, and deterioration from normal use, which a landlord is expected to absorb. The table gives typical examples, although every case turns on its own evidence and the length of the tenancy.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Usually a cleaning issue</th><th>Usually fair wear and tear</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Grease and burnt-on food inside the oven</td><td>Faded enamel or a worn oven door seal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Limescale and soap scum on taps and shower screens</td><td>Dulled chrome on older fittings</td></tr>
<tr><td>Food and drink stains on carpets</td><td>Flattened carpet pile in walkways</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dust and cobwebs on skirting boards and light fittings</td><td>Light scuffs on paintwork from furniture</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rubbish left in cupboards, the loft or the garden</td><td>Sun-faded curtains or blinds</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Where carpets were clean at check-in and now have spills or marks, a <a href="{{service:carpet-upholstery-cleaning}}">professional carpet clean</a> is usually a more proportionate fix than a claim towards replacement, and it is far easier for both sides to agree on.</p>
<h2>Common mistakes that lose cleaning disputes</h2>
<ul>
<li>No check-in inventory, or one that simply says "clean" for every item.</li>
<li>Photos with no dates, or check-out photos taken after contractors have already been in.</li>
<li>Claiming a flat sum for "cleaning" with no breakdown of what was done.</li>
<li>Tenants handing back keys before the cleaning is finished, or losing their cleaner's invoice.</li>
<li>Missing the scheme's deadlines for submitting evidence.</li>
<li>Arguing about general standards instead of comparing the property with the inventory.</li>
</ul>
<p>Knowing <a href="{{guide:what-letting-agents-check-at-check-out}}">what letting agents check at check-out</a> helps both sides focus on what an adjudicator will actually look at.</p>
<h2>The best evidence is a clean that passes first time</h2>
<p>A dispute takes weeks and leaves everyone frustrated. A clean that matches the inventory, with photos and an invoice to prove it, usually stops one before it starts. Our <a href="{{service:end-of-tenancy-cleaning}}">end of tenancy cleaning</a> follows letting-agent checklists, our cleaners are DBS-checked and insured, and if the agent flags something we missed, we come back and put it right.</p>
<p>We cover the whole region, including <a href="{{local:end-of-tenancy-cleaning-hull}}">end of tenancy cleaning in Hull</a> and <a href="{{local:end-of-tenancy-cleaning-beverley}}">Beverley</a>. Tell us your check-out date through our <a href="{{page:contact}}">contact page</a> and we will give you a fixed quote.</p>
